\ core::cmp -- the typed comparisons over the raw flag primitives.
\
\ The compiler is left with three machine-level primitives here: `branch`,
\ which jumps on a 32-bit flag and takes two quotations; `tag` (see
\ `core::bool`), which reads a payload-free enum's discriminant as that flag;
\ and the six comparison primitives `ueq`/`ult`/`ugt`/`ulte`/`ugte`/`une`,
\ which produce it. Everything typed -- `bool`, `if`, `eq` -- is written in
\ Sooth.
\
\ Each comparison is `'T: Copy Ord`-polymorphic, so one definition covers the
\ whole numeric tower rather than narrowing the language to `i64`, and each is
\ `inline`, so it is spliced at its call site: without that every comparison in
\ every program would become a real call with a frame. The branch-and-construct
\ body is also what makes an invalid `bool` impossible to build -- there is
\ deliberately no operation turning a machine word back into an enum, only
\ these two named variants.
import: intrinsics i | ueq ult ugt ulte ugte une branch | ;
import: self::bool b | Bool False True | ;

export: eq lt gt lte gte ne ;

: eq  inline ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool ) ueq  [ True ] [ False ] branch ;
: lt  inline ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool ) ult  [ True ] [ False ] branch ;
: gt  inline ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool ) ugt  [ True ] [ False ] branch ;
: lte inline ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool ) ulte [ True ] [ False ] branch ;
: gte inline ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool ) ugte [ True ] [ False ] branch ;
: ne  inline ( 'T: Copy Ord 'T -- Bool ) une  [ True ] [ False ] branch ;
